ring). Rewrite it to the code's own account: cardinality refuses the empty and multi-row shapes, and one deletion implies one source branch.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-144 Method -> before: a spine batch claims as one commit, and its worker receives both ids -> after: each spine claim carries one work item and its worker receives that item; a one-item parked lane resumes; a parked claim of several items stops the run before any mutation or relaunch; ordinary work admits after the exclusive turn. The rest is verbatim, exit 0, census dedupe and lanes=1 included -> not the same: the verified set changes. Ready to bless.\n- [MEANING+BLESS] TC-145 Method -> before: a spine batch claims as one commit whose subject joins the ids -> after: one spec claims in one commit whose subject names one id; an empty, separator-only or multi-item input is refused by name before the lock and any move, commit, branch or worktree; a historical joined claim commit stays readable and is never re-run. Every other arm is verbatim -> not the same: the verified set changes. Ready to bless. It drives the Done-when's empty-claim fail-open case.\n\n**What the act needs.**\n- The released rung lets the later act turn re-attest the sixteen `[MEANING+BLESS]` rows.\n- LLR-207 and TC-205 are not re-anchored, and they re-sit together.\n- No row here is on a held rung.\n- Parent-first: LLR-207's and TC-205's parents (SR-154 and SR-156) do not wait on them, and no blessed row here is a child of a returned row.\n\n**A note on the code, not on the rows.** In `tests/test_verdict_record.py`, the comment in `test_an_empty_close_is_refused_and_the_walk_covers_it_regardless` still says `len(branches) != 1` refuses the empty close first, and that the empty diff composes `adjudicate:  -> complete/ …`. Neither is true at this tip; the comment carries the same stale account as the two rows. It is a separate finding for the lane, not part of this verdict.\n\n### Dispositions (draft, for WI-977's own spec: returns answered